Z||000000||chris6878||02-13-2000||07:29 PM||||Hello everyone....   <P>Been a while since i posted, but...  lets get to the point...     i have a 85 fiero se with v6 (750 miles on the engine),  it starts up fine while cold... when it gets a little warm it doesnt  want to start,  and when it does start you have to  have the gas pedal to the floor..  but if it sits a while and cools, it will start fine.  Also, after it warms up while driving, it will studder  sometimes and ****  like its going to quit, but when u press the gas on down it will stop....        any suggestoins to what the problem could be... i already have a coil ordered and i am picking it up in the morning...  does this sound like the problem to u guys?  <P>thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>chris     ||||reg||5
Z||000002||fiero56||02-13-2000||07:54 PM||||I don't have the answer, but wanted to make sure everyone knows the starred out word is j-e-r-k. The forums software is setup to star out certain words and that is one of them.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mwbackus||02-13-2000||08:34 PM||||Hard warm-starting could be the oil pressure switch; it controls the fuel pump and only switches the pump on if the oil pressure is sufficient.  How is the oil pressure when hot at an idle?  Could also be leaking fuel injector(s), or a sticking cold start valve/switch.<P>Doubt that a bad coil would exhibit these symtoms, they usually effect top end performance when they are going bad, or quite completely.  You can easily check the coil resistance with a multimeter and determine if its weak (as instructed in a repair manual such as Haynes).<P>Shuddering and surge at normal cruise speed are usually caused by problems with the EGR system, although this may also be due to defects (eg. flat spots) in the TPS, a dirty or malfunctioning IAC valve (a slow reacting IAC will not usually set a code), dirty fuel injectors, bad spark plug wires, or incorrect ignition timing.<P>A $150 Autoxray ECM scanner is an invaluable tool for the diagnoses of problems such as these.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||02-13-2000||10:55 PM||||I have an 85SE v-6 4spd.  I rebuilt the engine about 500 miles ago.  It ran great until this week when it started shuddering at times.  I finally got a check engine light with 15 (coolant temp. sensor) and 35 (idle air control valve) codes.  Since I had changed the idle air control valve when I built the engine I changed the coolant temperature sensor.  This helped for about a day.  Again it started cutting out while driving along.  The idle would go haywire also when it was acting up.  The problem would come and go so I couldn't rely on the car for basic transportation.<P>I finally took it to the Pontiac dealer and he found a bad ECM.  $355 later and a new ECM and so far everything is fine.<P>When the ECM starts failing all kinds of things can happen.  It will start lieing about what is going on and set erroneous codes.<P>I don't know how to check to see if an ECM is bad.  Maybe someone else on this forum can help out here.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
