Z||000006||Shiner||02-13-2000||11:08 PM||||I have this EXACT same starting problem!!!!  The car runs and idles like a champ though.  Now, I KNOW the oil pressure sending unit is screwed up.  Do you really think this could be it?  I thought I had a bad injector or something!||||reg||8
Z||000007||DRH||02-14-2000||01:14 AM||||The warm start problem sounds like too much fuel. Holding to the floor puts the ECM into flood clear mode and cuts off most of the fuel. Most likely cause would probably be the cold start injector switch or the cold start injector. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||mwbackus||02-14-2000||01:17 AM||||When an oil pressure switch is bad, it will usually activate at abnormally high oil pressure.  This prevents the "instant" startup commonly experienced with a bone cold Fiero V6 (ie..higher oil pressure due to cold oil) the result is several added cranks needed to achieve the startup oil pressure with a warm engine.  Leaking fuel injectors are another common cause and this will result in very similar symptoms.  When the engine is shut off after sufficient operation (ie.. a short drive, etc.) a leaking injector will continue to deliver a slight amount of fuel into the intake (eg. the fuel rail stays pressurized for some time after shutdown).  When an attempt is made to start the motor before all this fuel has been evaporated (a long process), a flood condition exists which results in the excessive cranking nessecary to obtain start-up.<BR>I have to disagree about the ECM failure.  In my experience an ECM rarely fails without completely disabling the system. It is "impossible for the EPROM or fuel CALPAK data to be re-programmed by some unexplained event", resulting in discrepancies in evaluation logic.  A cleaning of the electrical harness contacts should be the only maintaince needed during its operational life. It is well known that some dealerships and shops will tack on a new ECM to a ticket when doing a simple repair, or even replace a perfectly good ECM when the problem eludes them.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000009||Shiner||02-14-2000||01:20 AM||||When I get the starting problem, nothing will start the car but about three seconds of cranking and a bit of gas.  I tried using the flood clear mode, no help.  The car SCARES me when I start it up cold.  You don't even get to hear the starter before it fires up!  It starts fine when it's hot too.  It's just when you let it sit for more than about 15 minutes.  I also get a trouble code when I am cruising at 60mph or over for a few miles.  The code is gone when I try to read it, and the light goes off when I restart the car.  ||||reg||5
Z||000010||Patrick||02-14-2000||01:38 AM||||<BR>Last summer there were a lot of threads here concerning hot starting problems. You might want to do a search on it. Here's one short thread I was involved in that might be of some help.<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/000072.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/000072.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||mwbackus||02-14-2000||02:25 AM||||Shiner:<BR>A code should set whenever the trouble light comes on during normal operating conditions (ie.. engine running, driving, etc.).  I have heard of cases where a defective ignition module would shut down the motor just for an instant.  On an auto trans car, this "shutdown" would be much more noticable, since a manual trans car would just resume operation after the failure condition had subsided.  In the later case, one may only notice a surge and a brief flash of the trouble light, but no code would be set.  Your symtoms sound more related to injector leakage than an oil pressure switch.  The pressure switch problem is notable in its repeatability when the motor is warm, but requires little soak time for the problem to occur(eg. a warm engine will retain very little oil pressure for a even a short duration after shutdown).  Useful information can be obtained by monitoring the exhaust when this condition occurs.  The initially rich exhaust upon startup caused by a leaking injector should be quite evident.  I do not understand how a defective MAP sensor could cause problems such as these.  Should not the output from this sensor be irrelevant due to the absence of engine vacuum during startup?  Perhaps I have overlooked something regarding the MAP sensors operating criterion, and I would welcome any theory that would explain this posibility.<P>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Shiner||02-14-2000||03:33 AM||||Nothing happens when the light comes on, it just lights up.  Stays on until the car is shutdown, then will not show again until I cruise fast.  All I got was code 12 when I tried to read it.  I also tried to start up the car in diagnostic mode, and it burned out the fuel pump fuse.  I should also note that the car gets VERY bad gas mileage.  You would think I had a 454 big block back there or something!||||reg||5
Z||000013||Alex4mula||02-14-2000||09:58 AM||||Mike;<P>Is the oil preassure switch that you mention the same as the oil preasure sending unit? The one that also controls the dial in the pod? I have en 88 Formula with that same problem. But also I think it may be too the started selenoid becasue it cranks very slow when hot (ie after running the engine a while). Worst may be a combination.||||reg||5
Z||000014||mwbackus||02-14-2000||09:59 AM||||The 12 flashes three times before the actual trouble codes are flashed.  You may want to check it again.<P>The oil pressure switch is on the same circuit as the fuel pump, and fuel pump relay. (This circuit is hot at all times)<P>The fuel pump fuse blowing out could be good indication that one of these components is operating incorrectly.  <BR>A short to ground in this circuit, or component failure, could cause the fuel pump to run at inappropriate times (battery drain could also occur).  If leakage is present in the system (injector, lines, or defective fuel canister) this could also result in VERY bad gas mileage.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by mwbackus (edited 02-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||mwbackus||02-14-2000||10:53 AM||||Alex:<BR>Yes, the oil pressure sender is actually a combination sender/switch, it has 3 terminals (instead of one).  It is referred to in a part database as "Oil Pressure Switch w/guage".  It contains a normally open switch which closes upon adequate oil pressure.  A total failure of this switch will prevent the motor from running (ie..no fuel). An important aspect of these type of sensors (eg. pressure transducers), is that they frequently become less sensitive to the intended stimulii after several years of use.<P>BTW, most GM starter designs that I know of have had severe operability problems caused by excessive heat, so it would not surprise me at all if you have issues in that area.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000016||Alex4mula||02-14-2000||11:19 AM||||Thank you Mike. I think I'll try a new starter first becasue of the slow cranking. If problem persist then I'll get a new Oil switch too. Hope not$... Oh well...||||reg||1
